# The step of a walk over a Map or a Set in fast code reads the keys of one width in line; a table with keys of the other width is for the stub that stands in front of the operation.
# A number never fits a short key, so the type of the keys chooses: numbers (beside undefined, null and booleans, which fit both) are read as wide keys, all else as short ones.
# The sizes tell the two forms apart: every walk over short keys is as long as the first one, every walk over wide keys as long as the second, and the two differ.
# FORM: Placed
# OPTIONS: heat=OnlyLoopsAreHot check=validate-back-end
# RUN: generate-code calls
# RUN(W): generate-code calls
# RUN(S): code-shapes

module m0 path="main.js" displayPath="main.js"
function f0 name="texts" parameters=[{type=Set<string>}, {type=anything}, {type=i32}] results=[{type=anything}] {
    Jump -> next b1()
  b1():
    LoopHint
    v4 = SetNext v0, v1, v2
    v5 = ResultAt v4
    v6 = ResultAt v4 index=2
    Branch v5 -> taken b1(), notTaken b2()
  b2():
    Return v6
}
# CHECK: f0: size=[[short]] frame=
# CHECK-NOT: size=[[short]] frame=
# S: f0 b1 Fast

function f1 name="numbers" parameters=[{type=Set<number>}, {type=anything}, {type=i32}] results=[{type=anything}] {
    Jump -> next b1()
  b1():
    LoopHint
    v4 = SetNext v0, v1, v2
    v5 = ResultAt v4
    v6 = ResultAt v4 index=2
    Branch v5 -> taken b1(), notTaken b2()
  b2():
    Return v6
}
# W: f1: size=[[wide]] frame=
# S: f1 b1 Fast

